  "worked_solution": "Let's go through each of the options.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is homogeneous of degree $2.$  It is a polynomial of degree $2,$ with no linear or constant terms.\n\n- The function $g(x,y)$ is not homogeneous. It is a polynomial of degree $3.$ However, it also contains a term $({y}^{2})$ that is of degree $2.$ Therefore, not all of its terms are of degree $3.$\n\n- The function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $3.$ It is a polynomial of degree $3,$ and all of its terms are of degree $3.$",
